  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nA large-scale cutoff low centered over the Great Plains with\nridging over the eastern seaboard will continue warm southwest\nflow into the region through Wednesday afternoon prior to the\ntrough/surface front arrival and its associated rain. The warm\nadvection combined with increasing cloud cover tonight may \nresult in record high minimum temperatures for Wednesday\nmorning, most likely at New Philadelphia, OH (KPHD) and\nWheeling, WV (KHLG) whose current records both sit at 59 \ndegrees.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nAs the aforementioned cutoff low transitions into an open-wave\ntrough over the Great Lakes during the next 24-36 hours, an\nassociated cold front will advance eastward towards the Ohio\nRiver Valley Wednesday afternoon and evening. Rain chances will\nincrease between 1pm and 4pm in eastern Ohio and 4pm and 9pm for\nnorthern West Virgina/western Pennsylvania, bringing an end to \nthe unusually prolonged dry stretch; the last measurable \nrainfall occurred on October 16th for Pittsburgh, PA. Rainfall \namounts are expected to range from 0.25 to 0.50 inches with this\nnew disturbance, with locally higher amounts of 0.50 to 0.75\ninches possible across isolated areas in eastern Ohio due to \nfavorable jet dynamics. Despite the anticipated rainfall, the \noverall flood threat remains low. The potential for lightning is\nalso low, but not zero, with any isolated strikes most likely \noccuring south of Pittsburgh between 4pm and 9pm Wednesday due \nto elevated instability with some noted dry air aloft and \nslightly higher dew points.\n\nBy early Thursday morning, a dry slot, will move into the \nregion from the west. Light rain and drizzle is possible near\ndawn. Otherwise, drier conditions will prevail into the late\nmorning. \n\nA brief period of dry and quiet weather is expected Thursday\nafternoon into early Friday. However, as the large-scale trough\nswings across Lake Erie, a secondary cold front will bring \nrenewed chances of lake-enhanced showers across the region. \n\nTemperatures will trend closer to seasonal averages during this\nperiod, with both daytime highs and overnight lows near normal.\nThe 850mb temperatures will fall from around +7 degrees Celsius\nearly Friday to -4 degrees Celsius during the late evening,\nsupporting a transition to colder conditions into the weekend.",
